1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,534
It's the end of an ordinary release week,
and your CTO asks: "Both pull requests

2
00:00:09,574 --> 00:00:14,607
were green. main is red. The two branches
did not touch a single file in common.

3
00:00:14,647 --> 00:00:14,924
How?"

4
00:00:15,264 --> 00:00:18,401
And a second one: "We reverted
the bad merge on Friday.

5
00:00:18,441 --> 00:00:22,847
On Monday we merged the repaired branch,
the merge was clean, and half of the

6
00:00:22,887 --> 00:00:24,984
feature is missing. Where did it go?"

7
00:00:27,324 --> 00:00:30,482
Neither needs a bug in Git. Each
follows from what a merge is.

8
00:00:30,522 --> 00:00:34,814
A merge is computed from exactly three
snapshots: your commit, their commit, and one

9
00:00:34,854 --> 00:00:39,146
common ancestor, called the merge base.
Git's own FAQ says it plainly: "Git does not

10
00:00:39,186 --> 00:00:43,426
consider the history or the individual
commits that have happened on those branches

11
00:00:43,466 --> 00:00:43,838
at all".

12
00:00:44,178 --> 00:00:50,065
The textbook quotes a 2024 poll in which 61
percent of roughly 1,480 respondents had

13
00:00:50,105 --> 00:00:55,145
seen a production bug caused by a bad
conflict resolution at least once.

14
00:00:55,185 --> 00:01:01,001
It's a self-selected sample, so read it as
direction, not as a population estimate.

15
00:01:01,041 --> 00:01:06,645
Part 2 starts by getting the foundation
right. Keep that Monday mystery in mind.

16
00:01:06,685 --> 00:01:07,633
It comes back.

17
00:01:09,473 --> 00:01:13,986
Welcome back to Git and GitHub Deep
Mastery. Pull up a chair: this is Part 2,

18
00:01:14,026 --> 00:01:18,657
integration and collaboration mechanics.
Everything in this part, merge, fetch,

19
00:01:18,697 --> 00:01:23,506
pull, push, rebase, cherry-pick, is explained
from the commit graph you learned in

20
00:01:23,546 --> 00:01:28,059
Part 1. As in Part 1, the commit IDs on
screen equal the IDs in the textbook,

21
00:01:28,099 --> 00:01:30,424
because the replays use the fixed clock.

22
00:01:30,764 --> 00:01:36,208
The sample project for the merge videos is
evalkit, a small evaluation harness for

23
00:01:36,248 --> 00:01:41,625
LLM outputs. Eval dot yaml, in the config
directory, holds the run configuration.

24
00:01:41,665 --> 00:01:45,905
Judge dot txt, in prompts, holds
the prompt for the judge model.

25
00:01:45,945 --> 00:01:49,918
And metrics dot py, in evalkit,
holds the scoring functions.

26
00:01:49,958 --> 00:01:53,195
You work on main. Asha and
Ravi work on branches.

27
00:01:53,535 --> 00:01:58,318
Two things today: the merge base, as
the third input of every merge, and the

28
00:01:58,358 --> 00:02:02,443
simplest merge there is, the
fast-forward, which creates nothing.

29
00:02:02,483 --> 00:02:07,648
Before every git merge in this video I'll
ask you the same question: fast-forward,

30
00:02:07,688 --> 00:02:10,821
true merge, or nothing?
You answer from the graph.

31
00:02:12,661 --> 00:02:14,239
Here's what you'll walk away with.

32
00:02:14,579 --> 00:02:15,991
After this video you can:

33
00:02:16,331 --> 00:02:18,617
Find the merge base of two commits, and
explain why it is the third input of a

34
00:02:18,657 --> 00:02:18,795
merge.

35
00:02:19,135 --> 00:02:22,151
Predict from the graph whether
git merge will fast-forward.

36
00:02:22,491 --> 00:02:25,387
List what a fast-forward changes in
.git, and what it does not create.

37
00:02:25,727 --> 00:02:28,239
Explain "Already up to
date" from ancestry.

38
00:02:30,079 --> 00:02:34,569
The merge base. In one sentence: the merge
base of two commits is their best common

39
00:02:34,609 --> 00:02:39,044
ancestor. That's the most recent commit
that both histories contain, and the third

40
00:02:39,084 --> 00:02:40,190
input of every merge.

41
00:02:40,530 --> 00:02:45,835
Precisely. A common ancestor of commits A
and B is a commit reachable from both by

42
00:02:45,875 --> 00:02:51,245
following parents. The manual defines the
rest: "One common ancestor is better than

43
00:02:51,285 --> 00:02:55,613
another common ancestor if the
latter is an ancestor of the former.

44
00:02:55,653 --> 00:03:00,828
A common ancestor that does not have any
better common ancestor is a best common

45
00:03:00,868 --> 00:03:06,238
ancestor, i.e. a merge base". Two branches
have diverged when each contains commits

46
00:03:06,278 --> 00:03:07,607
that the other lacks.

47
00:03:07,947 --> 00:03:11,670
Why is it an input? Without it,
you can't tell what each side did.

48
00:03:11,710 --> 00:03:16,401
If a line is present on one side and absent
on the other, was it added by the first

49
00:03:16,441 --> 00:03:20,106
or deleted by the second? Only the
common starting point decides.

50
00:03:20,446 --> 00:03:23,219
Four commands answer every
question about divergence.

51
00:03:23,259 --> 00:03:24,546
You met them in video 25.

52
00:03:24,886 --> 00:03:29,832
Which commit is the merge
base? git merge-base A B.

53
00:03:29,872 --> 00:03:37,459
Is A an ancestor of B? Add --is-ancestor:
exit status 0 means yes, 1 means no.

54
00:03:37,499 --> 00:03:43,130
How many commits does each side
have that the other lacks?

55
00:03:43,170 --> 00:03:49,192
git rev-list with --left-right,
--count, and A, three dots, B.

56
00:03:49,232 --> 00:03:57,014
Which commits are they? git log with
--oneline, --left-right, and the same three

57
00:03:57,054 --> 00:03:57,503
dots.

58
00:03:57,843 --> 00:04:02,701
Inside .git. Nothing is stored. The merge
base is computed from the parent lines of

59
00:04:02,741 --> 00:04:07,422
commit objects, every time a command needs
it. No file says where a branch "came

60
00:04:07,462 --> 00:04:07,776
from".

61
00:04:08,116 --> 00:04:13,266
Fast-forward. In one sentence: when your
current commit is an ancestor of the commit

62
00:04:13,306 --> 00:04:18,394
you merge, there's nothing to combine. So
Git moves your branch ref to that commit,

63
00:04:18,434 --> 00:04:20,000
and creates no new object.

64
00:04:20,340 --> 00:04:23,511
Precisely: the condition is git
merge-base --is-ancestor HEAD <other>.

65
00:04:23,551 --> 00:04:27,181
Put differently, the merge base is your
own tip. Fast-forwarding is the default.

66
00:04:27,521 --> 00:04:32,076
"Already up to date." The opposite
case has its own message.

67
00:04:32,116 --> 00:04:38,432
If the other commit is already an ancestor
of HEAD, the merge prints "Already up to

68
00:04:38,472 --> 00:04:44,711
date." and moves nothing. Hold on to that
message. It comes back at the end of the

69
00:04:44,751 --> 00:04:45,094
demo.

70
00:04:45,434 --> 00:04:49,049
So there are three cases, and
ancestry decides between them.

71
00:04:49,089 --> 00:04:53,983
HEAD is an ancestor of the other: fast-forward.
The other is an ancestor of HEAD:

72
00:04:54,023 --> 00:04:59,040
nothing to do. Neither: the branches have
diverged, and you get a true merge, which

73
00:04:59,080 --> 00:05:00,684
the next video takes apart.

74
00:05:01,024 --> 00:05:06,245
What a fast-forward changes in .git. The
file refs/heads/main is rewritten with the

75
00:05:06,285 --> 00:05:11,505
new commit ID. ORIG_HEAD receives the old
one. The reflogs of HEAD and of main each

76
00:05:11,545 --> 00:05:16,575
gain one line. The index and the working
tree are updated as by a branch switch.

77
00:05:16,615 --> 00:05:18,794
The object database doesn't change.

78
00:05:19,134 --> 00:05:25,634
One detail the textbook's authors found
while testing: every git merge call rewrites

79
00:05:25,674 --> 00:05:31,707
ORIG_HEAD with the tip it started from,
even a call that prints "Already up to

80
00:05:31,747 --> 00:05:37,936
date." or refuses. So as an undo pointer
it's valid only until the next attempt.

81
00:05:37,976 --> 00:05:40,505
The reflog is the durable record.

82
00:05:40,845 --> 00:05:45,390
Risk labels. git merge that fast-forwards
or makes a true merge is 🟡 CAUTION: it

83
00:05:45,430 --> 00:05:49,344
moves the current branch and rewrites
the index and the working tree.

84
00:05:49,384 --> 00:05:52,610
A git merge that answers
"Already up to date." is 🟢 SAFE.

85
00:05:55,950 --> 00:06:01,469
When a fast-forward is not what you
want. The risk is the missing trace.

86
00:06:01,509 --> 00:06:07,722
If somebody fast-forwards an unfinished
branch into main, the history looks as if

87
00:06:07,762 --> 00:06:13,897
the work-in-progress commits had been made
on main directly, and no merge commit

88
00:06:13,937 --> 00:06:19,147
exists to revert. Nobody wants to
discover that on a Friday evening.

89
00:06:19,187 --> 00:06:22,003
A later video shows how to forbid it.

90
00:06:23,843 --> 00:06:29,511
A picture helps. The textbook's analogy for
the merge base: two editors photocopy a

91
00:06:29,551 --> 00:06:33,500
manuscript on Monday, and mark
up their copies separately.

92
00:06:33,540 --> 00:06:38,727
On Friday you can combine their work
only if you still have the Monday copy.

93
00:06:38,767 --> 00:06:44,504
Without it, you can't tell a sentence that
one editor added from a sentence that the

94
00:06:44,544 --> 00:06:47,874
other deleted. The merge
base is the Monday copy.

95
00:06:48,214 --> 00:06:52,821
It breaks in two places. Git finds the
Monday copy by walking parent links, not by

96
00:06:52,861 --> 00:06:56,674
date. And a tangled history can have
two equally good Monday copies.

97
00:06:56,714 --> 00:06:57,921
That's the next video.

98
00:06:58,261 --> 00:07:02,062
And for the fast-forward: a bookmark in
a manuscript that somebody else kept

99
00:07:02,102 --> 00:07:06,258
writing. You move the bookmark to the last
page. It breaks on one point: nothing in

100
00:07:06,298 --> 00:07:10,505
the book records that the bookmark moved,
or that the new pages were ever a separate

101
00:07:10,545 --> 00:07:12,375
branch. Only your local reflog knows.

102
00:07:14,215 --> 00:07:15,453
Now with real commits.

103
00:07:15,793 --> 00:07:20,629
Two branches from one commit. main
has one commit of its own, c089834.

104
00:07:20,669 --> 00:07:26,272
feature/rationale has two. The commit under
the arrow, 6ae3c51, is reachable from

105
00:07:26,312 --> 00:07:30,521
both tips and is the most recent
such commit: the merge base.

106
00:07:30,561 --> 00:07:31,635
The Monday copy.

107
00:07:31,975 --> 00:07:37,906
Before: main points at the older commit,
and the feature branch is one commit

108
00:07:37,946 --> 00:07:44,341
further along the same line. After: the
label main has moved one step to the right.

109
00:07:44,381 --> 00:07:49,536
Count the commits in both halves:
two and two. Nothing was created.

110
00:07:49,576 --> 00:07:50,466
A ref moved.

111
00:07:52,306 --> 00:07:55,326
main has one commit of its own,
and feature/rationale has two.

112
00:07:55,366 --> 00:07:59,273
From the graph, name the merge base and
the two counts before the commands print

113
00:07:59,313 --> 00:07:59,520
them.

114
00:08:04,360 --> 00:08:09,142
The two counts read "one commit only on
the left side, two only on the right".

115
00:08:09,182 --> 00:08:14,273
In the --left-right log, the less-than sign
marks commits reachable only from main,

116
00:08:14,313 --> 00:08:18,663
and the greater-than sign marks commits
reachable only from the branch.

117
00:08:19,003 --> 00:08:23,255
Three dots mean something different to
git diff, and the difference matters for

118
00:08:23,295 --> 00:08:27,818
reviews. With three dots, git diff compares
the merge base with the branch tip: what

119
00:08:27,858 --> 00:08:30,643
the branch did, and nothing
that main did meanwhile.

120
00:08:30,683 --> 00:08:34,990
With two dots it compares the two tips,
so the temperature change that main made

121
00:08:35,030 --> 00:08:37,108
appears as if the branch had undone it.

122
00:08:39,948 --> 00:08:44,444
Two commits in a line. main is on
the older one. --is-ancestor main

123
00:08:44,484 --> 00:08:48,776
feature/batch-size exits with 0.
The repository has two commits.

124
00:08:49,116 --> 00:08:52,988
Fast-forward, true merge, or
nothing? Say it out loud.

125
00:08:53,028 --> 00:08:53,712
I'll wait.

126
00:08:58,552 --> 00:09:04,843
git merge 🟡 CAUTION.

127
00:09:05,183 --> 00:09:12,510
Fast-forward. "Updating 6ae3c51..23db174"
names the old and the new position of

128
00:09:12,550 --> 00:09:20,156
main. There are still two commits. The
reflog entry says merge feature/batch-size:

129
00:09:20,196 --> 00:09:27,617
Fast-forward, and ORIG_HEAD holds 6ae3c51,
the commit to return to if this was a

130
00:09:27,657 --> 00:09:28,363
mistake.

131
00:09:28,703 --> 00:09:33,223
Round two. Now main and a new
branch each gain one commit.

132
00:09:33,563 --> 00:09:38,279
Neither tip is an ancestor of the
other. The merge base is 23db174.

133
00:09:38,319 --> 00:09:40,906
Fast-forward, true merge, or nothing?

134
00:09:45,746 --> 00:09:52,280
A true merge. "Auto-merging config/eval.yaml"
reports a content merge: both sides

135
00:09:52,320 --> 00:09:57,718
changed that file, differently.
"Merge made by the 'ort' strategy."

136
00:09:57,758 --> 00:10:03,156
names the algorithm. The repository
went from four commits to five.

137
00:10:03,496 --> 00:10:09,071
The new commit has two parents. The
first parent is where you were, 017bef5.

138
00:10:09,111 --> 00:10:14,982
The second is what you merged, 67feed7.
The merged file carries temperature: 0.0

139
00:10:15,022 --> 00:10:20,376
from your side and timeout_s: 60 from
theirs. How did Git arrive at that?

140
00:10:20,416 --> 00:10:22,002
That's the next video.

141
00:10:22,342 --> 00:10:26,569
Last round. You merge feature/timeout
again, and then feature/batch-size.

142
00:10:26,609 --> 00:10:28,731
Fast-forward, true merge, or nothing?

143
00:10:33,571 --> 00:10:41,930
Nothing, twice: "Already up to date."
Both branch tips are ancestors of HEAD.

144
00:10:41,970 --> 00:10:50,437
That message is 🟢 SAFE: no ref moves.
Now, the message you were holding on to.

145
00:10:50,477 --> 00:10:59,162
It says the other commit is reachable from
yours. It does not say that the other

146
00:10:59,202 --> 00:11:07,997
branch's changes are present in your files.
That difference is the thread back to

147
00:11:08,037 --> 00:11:14,759
the Monday mystery, and the
challenge exercise is built on it.

148
00:11:16,599 --> 00:11:18,247
Five mistakes to watch for.

149
00:11:18,587 --> 00:11:24,527
Expecting a merge commit after "Fast-forward".
Root cause: the current tip was an

150
00:11:24,567 --> 00:11:30,654
ancestor of the other commit, so there was
nothing to combine; one ref moved and no

151
00:11:30,694 --> 00:11:32,057
object was written.

152
00:11:32,397 --> 00:11:35,900
Reading "Already up to date."
as "the change is in my files".

153
00:11:35,940 --> 00:11:40,779
Root cause: the message is a statement about
ancestry: the other commit is reachable

154
00:11:40,819 --> 00:11:41,360
from HEAD.

155
00:11:41,700 --> 00:11:46,232
Using two dots in git diff to review a
branch. Root cause: two dots compare the

156
00:11:46,272 --> 00:11:50,861
tips, so everything main did since the
fork appears reversed; three dots compare

157
00:11:50,901 --> 00:11:52,019
from the merge base.

158
00:11:52,359 --> 00:11:57,598
Relying on ORIG_HEAD after a second merge
attempt. Root cause: every git merge call

159
00:11:57,638 --> 00:12:02,814
rewrites ORIG_HEAD with the tip it started
from; the reflog is the durable record.

160
00:12:03,154 --> 00:12:06,343
Fast-forwarding an unfinished branch into
main. Root cause: the default allows it,

161
00:12:06,383 --> 00:12:07,919
and it leaves no merge commit to revert.

162
00:12:09,759 --> 00:12:14,131
Now, out of the lab. The textbook draws
two production consequences from the merge

163
00:12:14,171 --> 00:12:14,400
base.

164
00:12:14,740 --> 00:12:18,023
First, the three-dot diff is
what a pull request page shows.

165
00:12:18,063 --> 00:12:22,620
That's why a pull request doesn't list
changes that landed on the base branch after

166
00:12:22,660 --> 00:12:25,556
you forked. It's GitHub's
use of the same merge base.

167
00:12:25,896 --> 00:12:29,213
Second, the age of the merge
base predicts merge pain.

168
00:12:29,253 --> 00:12:34,186
A branch on an evaluation team that forked
three weeks ago is combined against a

169
00:12:34,226 --> 00:12:38,972
three-week-old snapshot, and everything
both sides did since then is in play.

170
00:12:39,012 --> 00:12:43,945
So for a branch that must live long,
merging main into it at intervals moves the

171
00:12:43,985 --> 00:12:46,805
merge base forward and
keeps each merge small.

172
00:12:47,144 --> 00:12:50,394
And the fast-forward in daily life? It's
what git pull does on a branch where you

173
00:12:50,434 --> 00:12:51,531
have no commits of your own.

174
00:12:53,371 --> 00:13:00,034
Your turn. Do Lab 6.1, "Predict
fast-forward or three-way", in

175
00:13:00,074 --> 00:13:02,629
lab-manual/m06-merge.md.

176
00:13:02,969 --> 00:13:11,136
For each merge in the lab, write down before
you run it: fast-forward, true merge,

177
00:13:11,176 --> 00:13:18,441
or nothing. The merge base. And how
many commits the repository will have

178
00:13:18,481 --> 00:13:21,744
afterwards. Then check all three.

179
00:13:23,584 --> 00:13:28,903
Q123: "git merge feature printed "Fast-forward".
What changed in .git, and what did

180
00:13:28,943 --> 00:13:29,226
not?"

181
00:13:31,566 --> 00:13:36,472
Answer out loud. A strong answer goes
through the dot git folder systematically:

182
00:13:36,512 --> 00:13:40,924
refs, the special ref that records
the old tip, reflogs, index, objects.

183
00:13:40,964 --> 00:13:45,932
It says what is absent from the list and
why. And it says what that absence means

184
00:13:45,972 --> 00:13:50,631
for someone who later wants to undo the
merge, or see in the history that it

185
00:13:50,671 --> 00:13:51,188
happened.

186
00:13:53,028 --> 00:13:57,614
Let's land this. You should now be able
to say: the merge base is the best common

187
00:13:57,654 --> 00:14:00,985
ancestor of two commits and the
third input of every merge.

188
00:14:01,025 --> 00:14:05,669
It's computed from parent links, not stored.
If my tip is an ancestor of the other

189
00:14:05,709 --> 00:14:10,238
commit, git merge fast-forwards: the branch
ref moves, ORIG_HEAD and the reflogs

190
00:14:10,278 --> 00:14:12,295
record it, and no object is created.

191
00:14:12,635 --> 00:14:14,879
If the other commit is an ancestor of
mine, Git says "Already up to date."

192
00:14:14,919 --> 00:14:17,225
and moves nothing. If neither is an
ancestor of the other, the branches have

193
00:14:17,265 --> 00:14:19,016
diverged, and the merge creates
a commit with two parents.

194
00:14:20,856 --> 00:14:24,551
Read sections 8.1 to 8.3 of Chapter 8.

195
00:14:24,891 --> 00:14:30,803
Do Exercise 6.1, Level 1, "a
fast-forward, then a merge commit", in

196
00:14:30,843 --> 00:14:33,734
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

197
00:14:34,074 --> 00:14:36,460
Challenge: Exercise 6.7, Level 3, "Already
up to date, and the change is not there",

198
00:14:36,500 --> 00:14:36,951
in the same file.

199
00:14:37,291 --> 00:14:42,078
Today you predicted every merge from
the graph, before Git answered.

200
00:14:42,118 --> 00:14:45,911
If the merge base still feels
slippery, that's normal.

201
00:14:45,951 --> 00:14:51,093
Next time we take the true merge apart:
three inputs, one rule table, and

202
00:14:51,133 --> 00:14:56,417
criss-cross histories. Until then, look
at the state first and type second.

203
00:14:56,457 --> 00:14:58,120
See you in the next one.
